<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sergei Eisenstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sergei Eisenstein was a Soviet film director and theorist whose use of montage made him one of the most influential figures in the history of cinema. His films include Battleship Potemkin (1925), October (1928), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> (1938), celebrating the victory on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peipus">Lake Peipus</a>, and Ivan the Terrible, whose second part was banned by Stalin. His writings on film form remain widely studied.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sergei-eisenstein.jpg" alt="Sergei Eisenstein" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sergei_Eisenstein_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
